{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ObtuseTiling","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ObtuseTiling","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ObtuseWedgeDegenerate","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetObtuseReduction","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetEqChimney","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.MostowIdealTetVolume"],"declarations":25,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_unconditional","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_unconditional","kind":"theorem","line":867,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L867-L923","doc":"**MILNOR'S IDENTITY, UNCONDITIONAL.**\n\n    Vol(idealTetrahedron P₁ P₂ P₃) = Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)\n\nfor EVERY positive triple summing to `π` — no acuteness, no distinguished angle,\nno case hypothesis at all.  The region is the independently-defined one (four\ngeodesic half-spaces from the four ideal vertices).\n\nThe proof is a four-way split.  All three acute is\n`MostowIdealTetVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices` (File A4).  `γ` non-acute is\n`idealTetVolume_of_vertices_nonacute`.  The other two are that same theorem after\na cyclic relabel, which `idealTetrahedron_cyclic` and `circlePoint_add_two_pi`\nshow is the identity on the region.  `at_most_one_obtuse` is not needed: the\nsplit is by cases rather than by exclusion, and a triple with two non-acute\nangles simply lands in a branch whose hypotheses `linarith` refutes.\n\nClosed modulo the single cited totally-geodesic / normalized-position\nidentification (File A1's `geodesicPlaneThrough`), which the acute theorem\nalready rested on; the non-acute cases add nothing to it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_nonacute","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_nonacute","kind":"theorem","line":856,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L856-L865","doc":"**The non-acute case in one statement** — obtuse and right-angled together,\n`π/2 ≤ γ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.circlePoint_add_two_pi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.circlePoint_add_two_pi","kind":"theorem","line":850,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L850-L854","doc":"`circlePoint` is `2π`-periodic — the step that turns the third relabelled\nvertex `t + 2γ + 2α + 2β` back into the first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetrahedron_cyclic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetrahedron_cyclic","kind":"theorem","line":838,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L838-L848","doc":"**The ideal tetrahedron is invariant under cycling its vertices.**  Its three\nvertical faces are the same three half-spaces, listed in a different order, once\n`verticalHalfSpaceThrough_swap` is used to align the two that appear with their\nedge reversed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.verticalHalfSpaceThrough_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.verticalHalfSpaceThrough_swap","kind":"theorem","line":830,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L830-L836","doc":"**The oriented half-space does not care which end of its edge is first.**\nBoth factors of `sarea b c i * sarea b c (p 0, p 1)` flip under the swap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":779,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L779-L806","doc":"**MILNOR'S IDENTITY FOR AN OBTUSE IDEAL TETRAHEDRON.**\n\n    Vol(idealTetrahedron P₁ P₂ P₃) = Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)      (π/2 < γ)\n\nthe obtuse counterpart of `idealTetVolume_of_vertices`.  The region is the\nindependently-defined one — the intersection of four geodesic half-spaces built\nfrom the four ideal vertices — not a chimney by fiat.\n\nTogether with the acute theorem this covers **every** admissible triple except\nthe right-angled one, where `γ = π/2` degenerates the subtracted wedge to a\nhalf-plane and the `ψ < π/2` wedge machinery fails on both sides.\n\n`Λ(γ) < 0` here, which is Milnor's \"one piece enters with a minus sign\"; the\nsubtraction is realised set-theoretically by `obtuseWedges_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle`\nand is carried out in `ℝ`, never in `ℝ≥0∞`.\n\nClosed modulo the same single cited totally-geodesic / normalized-position\nidentification the acute theorem rests on (File A1's `geodesicPlaneThrough` — a\ndefinition with a citation, not an axiom or `sorry`).  No analytic continuation\nis used anywhere, and acuteness of `α` and `β` is derived rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_right","kind":"theorem","line":750,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L750-L760","doc":"**MILNOR'S IDENTITY FOR A RIGHT-ANGLED IDEAL TETRAHEDRON**, on the\nvertex-defined region.  With this the acute, obtuse and right-angled theorems\ncover the WHOLE open angle simplex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle_right","kind":"theorem","line":717,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L717-L748","doc":"**MILNOR'S Λ-SUM ON A RIGHT-ANGLED TRIANGLE.**  `Λ(π/2) = 0`, so the\nidentity is just the two wedge volumes — which is what \"nothing to subtract\"\nmeans analytically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.union_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.union_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle_right","kind":"theorem","line":686,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L686-L715","doc":"**THE RIGHT-ANGLED TILING.**  At `γ = π/2` the triangle is a.e. the union of\nthe two surviving wedges — no subtraction, and only ONE null set (the single edge\nline the covering needs), because both chord directions are strict here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.gamma_chord_of_mem_beta_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.gamma_chord_of_mem_beta_right","kind":"theorem","line":666,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L666-L684","doc":"**The `γ`-chord for a point of `W_β`, at `γ = π/2`** — the mirror, from the\nFIRST certificate and `W_β`'s upper edge (`perp_t < 0` after the `2π` shift). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.gamma_chord_of_mem_alpha_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.gamma_chord_of_mem_alpha_right","kind":"theorem","line":645,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L645-L664","doc":"**The `γ`-chord for a point of `W_α`, at `γ = π/2`** — strict, and with no\nappeal to `W_sub` (which is empty here).  The second certificate at `cos γ = 0`\nreads `perp_{t+2γ}·sin β = cos α·(γ-chord slack)`, and `W_α`'s lower edge gives\n`perp_{t+2γ} > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.wedgeAt_pi_div_two_eq_empty","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.wedgeAt_pi_div_two_eq_empty","kind":"theorem","line":634,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L634-L643","doc":"**A half-angle of `π/2` gives the EMPTY wedge**, not a half-plane.\n\n`Real.tan (π/2) = 0` by Lean's division-by-zero convention, so the wedge's\nangular condition is `|⟨w,v_θ⟩| < 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":501,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L501-L600","doc":"**MILNOR'S Λ-SUM ON AN OBTUSE TRIANGLE.**\n\n    Vol(chimney (inscribedTriangle R t α β γ) R)  =  Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)\n\nfor `π/2 < γ` — the obtuse counterpart of `hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle`,\nwhose hypotheses are `α, β, γ < π/2`.  Together the two cover every triangle\nexcept the right-angled one, where `γ = π/2` makes `W_sub` degenerate to a\nhalf-plane and both statements' wedge machinery (`ψ < π/2`) fails.\n\n`Λ(γ)` is negative here — `γ` obtuse puts `Λ(γ) = −Λ(π−γ)` with `π−γ` acute —\nwhich is the analytic face of Milnor's \"one piece enters with a minus sign\".\nThe identity is the same one the acute case proves; only its realisation changes.\n\nNote that no analytic continuation is used, and none of the three angles is\nrequired to be acute in the *statement*: acuteness of `α` and `β` is derived\nfrom `γ` obtuse via `acute_of_obtuse_third`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle","kind":"theorem","line":451,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L451-L482","doc":"**THE OBTUSE TILING.**  Milnor's obtuse figure is the inscribed triangle, up\nto a null set — the counterpart of `milnorWedges_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle`.\n\nThe two directions are controlled by two *different* null sets, and neither is\navoidable:\n\n* `obtuseWedges \\ T` sits in the `γ`-chord **line**, an AFFINE line (it does not\n  pass through the circumcentre, since `R cos γ ≠ 0`), killed by\n  `volume_chordLine`.  `volume_perpLine` cannot reach it.\n* `T \\ obtuseWedges` sits in the single edge line `perp_{t+2γ+2α} = 0`, through\n  the origin, killed by `volume_perpLine`.  The acute statement needs three such\n  lines here; the obtuse covering needs one.\n\n`T ∩ W_sub = ∅` is strict (`notMem_sub_of_mem_inscribedTriangle`), so the\nsubtraction costs no null set of its own. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges_subset_inscribedTriangle_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges_subset_inscribedTriangle_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":419,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L419-L449","doc":"**The reverse inclusion.**  A point of the obtuse figure off the `γ`-chord\nLINE lies in the triangle.\n\nIts own wedge supplies one chord directly and the neighbouring one through\n`wedgeAt_lt_chord_next` / `_prev` — both usable because `α` and `β` are acute.\nThe `γ`-chord is the one that cannot be proved directly (`γ` obtuse puts\n`R cos γ < 0`, outside every chord helper's range, and `W_α ⊄ chordHalfplane R\n(t+γ) γ` in fact); it is extracted from `w ∉ W_sub` by\n`gamma_chord_of_mem_alpha` / `_beta`, which conclude `≤`.  Ruling out the\nequality case is exactly the hypothesis `hchord`, and is why this direction is\na.e. rather than strict. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measurableSet_obtuseWedges","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measurableSet_obtuseWedges","kind":"theorem","line":414,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L414-L417","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.obtuseWedges","kind":"def","line":402,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L402-L412","doc":"**Milnor's obtuse figure**: the two surviving wedges, minus the one the\nobtuse angle turns subtractive.\n\nThe acute `milnorWedges` is a union of three; here the `γ`-wedge is replaced by\n`W_sub = wedgeAt R (t+γ+π) (π−γ)`, which sits on the far side of the `γ`-chord\nand enters with a minus sign.  This is a set difference, not a signed sum: the\nsubtracted wedge is contained in the union up to the chord line, so the\narithmetic of Milnor's signed subdivision is realised set-theoretically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.wedgeAt_sub_subset_alphaBeta_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.wedgeAt_sub_subset_alphaBeta_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":325,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L325-L398","doc":"**The subtracted wedge sits inside the union it is subtracted from** — off\nthe same single edge line.\n\nWithout this the set difference `(W_α ∪ W_β) \\ W_sub` would be arithmetically\nsmaller than Milnor's signed sum `W_α + W_β − W_sub`: subtracting a set removes\nonly the part that was there.  With it, the two agree, and the volume identity\ncan be read off by additivity rather than by a signed-measure argument.\n\nThe proof is the two certificates read BACKWARDS.  `W_sub` is an *acute* wedge —\nits half-angle is `π − γ ∈ (0, π/2)` precisely because `γ` is obtuse — so\n`wedgeAt_lower` / `_upper` give it the same two cone bounds the triangle has,\n`perp_{t+2γ} > 0` and `perp_t < 0`.  What it has instead of the triangle's\n`γ`-chord is the OPPOSITE inequality `⟨w,u_{t+γ}⟩ > R cos γ`, and feeding that\nsign into\n\n    cos γ·(α-chord slack) = cos α·(γ-chord slack) − perp_{t+2γ}·sin β\n    cos γ·(β-chord slack) = perp_t·sin α + cos β·(γ-chord slack)\n\nmakes both left sides negative, hence — `cos γ` being negative — both chord\nslacks positive.  So a point of `W_sub` satisfies the `α`- and `β`-chords even\nthough it fails the `γ`-chord, which is exactly the geometry of a circumcentre\nthat has left the triangle across one edge. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.inscribedTriangle_subset_obtuseWedges_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.inscribedTriangle_subset_obtuseWedges_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L291-L323","doc":"**THE OBTUSE COVERING.**  A point of the triangle off the single edge line\n`perp_{t+2γ+2α} = 0` lies in `W_α` or in `W_β`.\n\nBoth cone bounds are already established, so the case split is on that one\nfunctional and each branch is one application of `mem_wedgeAt_of_halfplanes`\n(available because `α` and `β` are acute — which is what `γ` obtuse buys).\n\nCompare the acute statement, which needs all THREE edge functionals nonzero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_second_pos_of_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_second_pos_of_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L265-L289","doc":"**The triangle lies strictly above the second vertex ray** — the mirror,\nfrom `perp_second_certificate` with `α` acute in place of `β`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_start_neg_of_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_start_neg_of_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L233-L263","doc":"**The triangle lies strictly below the first vertex ray.**\n\n`cos γ < 0` is the whole content: it flips the first term of\n`perp_start_certificate`, and the second is negative anyway because `β` is\nacute.  In the acute case both terms have the opposite sign and the conclusion\nis false — the circumcentre is then inside the triangle and `perp_t` takes both\nsigns on it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_second_certificate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_second_certificate","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L214-L231","doc":"**Certificate for the second edge functional**, the mirror at\n`V₂ = R·u_{t+2γ}`, against the `γ`-chord `V₁V₂` and the `α`-chord `V₂V₃`.\n\nSame elimination in the frame at `s = t + 2γ`, where the two chords sit at\n`s − γ` and `s + α`; it yields `perp_s·sin(γ+α) = F cos α − H cos γ` with\n`H := R cos α − ⟨w,u_{s+α}⟩`, and `sin(γ+α) = sin β`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_start_certificate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.perp_start_certificate","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L188-L212","doc":"**Certificate for the first edge functional**, against the two chords that\nmeet at the triangle's first vertex `V₁ = R·u_t`.\n\nWriting `P = R − ⟨w,u_t⟩` and `Q = perp_t(w)`, the shift identity\n`⟨w,u_{t+φ}⟩ = ⟨w,u_t⟩ cos φ + Q sin φ` turns the two chord slacks into\n\n    F := R cos γ − ⟨w,u_{t+γ}⟩   = P cos γ − Q sin γ      (the γ-chord, V₁V₂)\n    G := R cos β − ⟨w,u_{t−β}⟩   = P cos β + Q sin β      (the β-chord, V₃V₁)\n\nand eliminating `P` gives `Q·sin(β+γ) = G cos γ − F cos β`, which is the\nstatement below once `sin(β+γ) = sin α` and `t + 2γ + 2α + β = t − β + 2π`.\nNote `R` cancels: both sides are `R`-free, and the identity holds for every `w`\nand every angle triple summing to `π`, acute or not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.notMem_sub_of_mem_inscribedTriangle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.notMem_sub_of_mem_inscribedTriangle","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L128-L148","doc":"**A point of the triangle is never in the subtracted wedge** — and this\ndirection is STRICT, no null set needed.\n\n`W_sub`'s chord condition is the strict complement of the triangle's `γ`-chord\n(see `gamma_chord_of_notMem_sub`), so membership in the triangle refutes it\noutright.  This is the cheap half of `T ⊆ RHS`; the other half is the covering\nargument, which does need the edge lines. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.volume_chordLine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.volume_chordLine","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ObtuseTiling.lean#L90-L126","doc":"**A chord line is null.**  The companion of `volume_perpLine` for an\n*affine* line: `{w | ⟨w,u_φ⟩ = c}` rather than `{w | ⟨w,v_φ⟩ = 0}`.\n\nTwo observations make it a corollary rather than a new measure-theoretic\nargument.  First, the corresponding line **through the origin** is already a\n`perpLine`, at the rotated angle `φ + π/2`:\n\n    −⟨w, (sin(φ+π/2), −cos(φ+π/2))⟩ = −(w₁ cos φ + w₂ sin φ),\n\nso it vanishes exactly where the chord functional does.  Second, the level set\nat height `c` is that kernel translated by `c · u_φ`, and `volume` on `ℝ × ℝ` is\ntranslation invariant.  (Mathlib's `Measure.addHaar_affineSubspace` would also\nserve, at the cost of packaging the set as an `AffineSubspace`.) ","signatureChunk":0}]}